  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 136 AM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nToday there is an upper-level ridge over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana with strong southwesterly flow aloft. This will \nbring warm (well-above seasonal average) temperatures to the area \nwith mostly dry weather. On Tuesday the upper-level ridge \ntransitions to weak zonal flow (west-east) aloft over North-central \nand Central Montana with southerly flow aloft over Southwestern \nMontana. This will cool down temperatures to near seasonal averages. \nDue to some moisture moving over Southwestern Montana combined with \nweak southerly flow aloft there is a low chance for showers \nacross Southwestern Montana on Tuesday. See the Forecast \nConfidence & Scenarios section for details. It will be dry across \nNorth-central and Central Montana on Tuesday.\n\nOn Wednesday over Southwestern Montana there remains Southwest flow \naloft with weak zonal flow (west-east) over North-central, and \nCentral Montana. Due to slightly stronger warm air advection on \nWednesday temperatures will rebound to above seasonal averages on \nWednesday. Across North-central and Central Montana it will be dry \nwith a low chance for showers across Southwestern Montana on \nWednesday. See the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios Section for \ndetails. On Thursday there remains southerly flow aloft and an upper-\nlevel trough associated with a Pacific Cold Front begins to move \nover North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana by Thursday \nevening. This will allow temperatures to remain above seasonal \naverages with mostly dry weather until Thursday evening. Thursday \nevening into Friday morning precipitation will gradually spread \nover North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \n\nOn Friday the upper-level trough merges with a shortwave trough \nmoving south from Canada. This will bring widespread precipitation \nand cooler temperatures to the area. Due to the cold front that \nmoved through Thursday evening into Friday morning snow-levels will \nlower and the precipitation that falls in mountain areas on Friday \nwill be snow. On Friday there is an increasing pressure gradient and \nstrong low-level and mid-level winds. This will bring windy \ncondtions to North-central Montana on Friday. On Saturday an upper-\nlevel ridge begins to build in over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will allow the area to dry out with cool \ntemperatures lingering. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nTuesday through Wednesday across Southwestern Montana along the \nIdaho/Montana Border there is a 10% chance for a tenth of rain or \ngreater. On Friday there is a 40 - 65% chance for wind gusts of 45 \nmph or greater across the Rocky Mountain Front, Hi-Line, Judith \nBasin and Fergus Counties. \n\nFor total liquid precipitation Thursday night through Saturday \nmorning there is a 50 - 80% chance for one half inch of liquid \nprecipitation or greater across most of Central and Southwestern \nMontana. For total liquid precipitation Thursday night through \nSaturday morning there is a 25 - 50% chance for a quarter inch of\nliquid precipitation across North-central Montana. For total \nliquid precipitation Thursday night through Saturday morning there\nis a 50 - 80% chance for one inch of liquid precipitation or \ngreater across the Madison and Gallatin ranges and the Little \nBelts. Across most of the mountains of North-central, Central, and\nSouthwestern Montana there is greater than a 60% chance for 2 \ninches of snow or greater from Thursday night through Saturday \nmorning. Thursday night through Saturday morning there is a 50 - \n70% chance for 4 inches of snow or greater across the Madison and \nGallatin ranges. -IG",
